{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YHCKQvNSuxdKonv3Vwnjfq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Soundness of the customer search\n2\t\n3\tSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4). Every certified\n4\trefutation in the corpus rests on one claim: that the pruning rules of Chu &\n5\tStuckey's (2009) customer search never discard the last solution. Two\n6\t`better_move` bugs broke that claim in code, and testing caught both\n7\t(`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §1–§3 and §7). This document states the claim\n8\tprecisely and then proves it.\n9\t\n10\t- §1 models the search as a mathematical object.\n11\t- §2 states each rule *as the fixed code implements it*, quotes the code lines,\n12\t  and says which composition of rules is claimed sound.\n13\t- §3 (item 06) checks every statement by brute force.\n14\t- §4 (items 07–11) gives the Lean proofs.\n15\t- §5 (item 12) states the theorem and maps each premise the certificate checker\n16\t  verifies to the Lean lemma that justifies it.\n17\t\n18\t§1–2 were written in loop0006 item 05 and contain no Lean.\n19\t\n20\t**Code references** are to the tree at commit `8d824d034`:\n21\t`satisfiability/customer_search.py` (**Py**, the reference implementation),\n22\t`satisfiability/customer_search.c` (**C**), and\n23\t`learning/search_certificate.py` (**Cert**, the emitter and the independent\n24\tchecker). Line numbers are given as `Py:238–252`. Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009)\n25\tare those of `literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`, the preprint, cited as \"PDF p.\".\n26\t\n27\t**Status of the statements.** Every conclusion in §2 was spot-checked before\n28\tit was written down, using a throwaway script (not item 06's deliverable).\n29\tThe script computes the predicate `Sol` of §1.4 exactly by dynamic programming\n30\tover closed sets, on 300 random graphs with 2–8 vertices at every `k`, 1,461\n31\t`(graph, k)` pairs in all. The checks were:\n32\t\n33\t- Lemma F (free moves);\n34\t- the conclusion of the definite move at every playable `q` meeting its\n35\t  premise, in every state `cl(T)` with `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`;\n36\t- the conclusion of the subset rule for every ordered pair;\n37\t- the corrected better move for every ordered pair.\n38\t\n39\tThere were zero failures. This is evidence, not proof: the statements are\n40\tclaims until items 06–12 settle them.\n41\t\n42\t---\n43\t\n44\t## 1. The search as a mathematical object\n45\t\n46\t### 1.1 Instance, customers, neighbourhoods\n47\t\n48\tAn instance is a 0/1 matrix `M` with rows as customers and columns as products.\n49\tThe search sees only the **active** customers `C`, those with at least one\n50\tproduct (`Py:225`). A customer with no product never opens a stack, and the\n51\tsearch leaves it out of the witness (`Py:193–194`). On `C`, `N[c]` is the\n52\tself-inclusive neighbourhood in the MOSP graph: `d ∈ N[c]` iff `d = c` or some\n53\tproduct is needed by both (`Py:224`, `_neighbour_masks`; Cert has its own\n54\t`neighbourhoods`, which shares no code with the search).\n55\t\n56\tChu & Stuckey observe (2009, §2, PDF p. 4) that the products matter only through\n57\tthis graph: \"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the\n58\tsame minimum number of stacks\". So everything below is a statement about a\n59\tfinite graph `G` on `C` and an integer `k ≥ 0`.\n60\t\n61\t### 1.2 Opened sets and the quantities the rules use\n62\t\n63\tFor `T ⊆ C`:\n64\t\n65\t| symbol | definition | code |\n66\t|---|---|---|\n67\t| `O(T)` | `⋃_{c ∈ T} N[c]`, the stacks *opened* so far (`O(∅) = ∅`) | `opened` |\n68\t| `fin(X)` | `{c ∈ C : N[c] ⊆ X}`, the customers finished once `X` is open | `free_moves`, Py:238–252 |\n69\t| `o(c, T)` | `N[c] ∖ O(T)`, what closing `c` next would newly open | `opens[c]`, Py:285–291; C:431–441 |\n70\t| `open(c, T)` | `|o(c, T)|` | `own.bit_count()`, `sizes[j]` |\n71\t| `close(c, T)` | `|{d ∈ C ∖ fin(O(T)) : o(d, T) ⊆ o(c, T)}|` | Py:408, C:320–323 |\n72\t\n73\tTwo facts are used everywhere, and both are immediate:\n74\t\n75\t- `T ⊆ fin(O(T))`;\n76\t- `O(fin(O(T))) = O(T)`. Every `N[c]` with `c ∈ fin(O(T))` lies inside `O(T)`,\n77\t  and `T ⊆ fin(O(T))` gives the reverse inclusion.\n78\t\n79\t`close` is Chu & Stuckey's count (PDF p. 4), \"the number of new stacks that will\n80\tclose\", with one difference. The paper's `d` ranges over every customer, while\n81\tthe code's ranges only over customers not yet finished, the `d` with\n82\t`o(d, T) ≠ ∅`. The code closes every finished customer before any rule runs\n83\t(§1.3), so at the states where the rules run the two ranges differ only by\n84\tcustomers already closed. `close(c, T)` counts `c` itself whenever\n85\t`o(c, T) ≠ ∅`.\n86\t\n87\t### 1.3 Moves, costs, and free-closed states\n88\t\n89\t**A move** closes one customer. At closed set `T`, closing `c ∉ T` costs\n90\t\n91\t    cost(T, c) = |O(T ∪ {c}) ∖ T| = |O(T) ∖ T| + open(c, T),\n92\t\n93\tthe stacks open at the moment `c` closes, counting `c` itself (Py:17–22,\n94\tPy:321; C:275–287 computes it in the second form, as `open_now + sizes[j]`).\n95\tThis is the paper's `|O(S) − S ∪ o(c, S)|` (PDF p. 4). The move is **playable**\n96\twhen `cost(T, c) ≤ k`. A **closing order from `T`** is an enumeration\n97\t`c₁, …, c_m` of `C ∖ T`, and its cost is `max_i cost(T ∪ {c₁, …, c_{i−1}}, c_i)`\n98\t(0 when `m = 0`).\n99\t\n100\t**The plain predicate.** `P_k(T)` means that some closing order from `T` has\n101\tcost at most `k`.\n102\t\n103\t**Free-closed states.** The search never stops at an arbitrary `T`. On entering\n104\ta node it closes every remaining customer whose neighbourhood is already open\n105\t(the *free moves*, Py:259–266 and C:431–448). A free move opens nothing, and\n106\topening nothing creates no new free moves, so one pass suffices (Py:241–243).\n107\tThe node's closed set is therefore\n108\t\n109\t    cl(T) = fin(O(T)),\n110\t\n111\tand by §1.2 the opened set `O(cl(T)) = O(T)` is a function of the closed set.\n112\tThis is why the memo can key on the closed set alone (Py:148–150, C:451). A\n113\t**state** is a free-closed set `S = cl(S)`. Its **remaining** customers are\n114\t`R(S) = C ∖ S`, and each has `o(c, S) ≠ ∅`. The **child** of `S` by `c ∈ R(S)`\n115\tis `S·c = cl(S ∪ {c})`. The search calls itself on `closed | bit(c)`, and the\n116\tcallee closes the free moves (Py:364, C:482).\n117\t\n118\t**The search's predicate.** For a state `S`, `Sol_k(S)` holds iff `S = C`, or\n119\tsome `c ∈ R(S)` has `cost(S, c) ≤ k` and `Sol_k(S·c)`.\n120\t\n121\tThe root is `∅ = cl(∅)`, because every `N[c]` is nonempty.\n122\t\n123\t### 1.4 The decision question\n124\t\n125\tThe search answers the question: **is there a closing order of cost ≤ k from\n126\t∅?** When the cost cut is its only pruning, it returns `sat` exactly when\n127\t`Sol_k(∅)`. The whole point of §2 is to show that the rules do not change this\n128\tanswer.\n129\t\n130\tTwo facts connect this question to MOSP. Both are for items 07 and 12 to prove\n131\tin Lean.\n132\t\n133\t**Lemma F (free moves).** Suppose `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`. Then\n134\t`P_k(T) ⇔ Sol_k(cl(T))`.\n135\t\n136\t*Why.* (⇒) In any closing order from `T`, a customer `f ∈ fin(O(T)) ∖ T` can be\n137\tmoved to the front. That changes no opened set, and every step before `f`'s old\n138\tposition gets cheaper by one, because `f ∈ O(T)` was counted as open there.\n139\t(⇐) Closing the free customers one at a time costs at most `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`\n140\teach. The hypothesis holds at the root (`0`) and at every child, where it is\n141\tthe parent's step cost minus one. So it holds at every node the search visits.\n142\t\n143\t**Cost = out-narrowness = pathwidth + 1.** Take a full closing order\n144\t`τ = c₁, …, c_n` from `∅`, and write `S_i = {c₁, …, c_i}`. Its `i`-th step costs\n145\t`|N[S_i] ∖ S_{i−1}|`. That is `outShackBeforeMove τ (i−1)` of Kornai & Tuza's\n146\tdual shack process, formalised in `Complex/Narrowness.lean` (`EnteredBy`,\n147\t`outShackBeforeMove`). The shack just after the move is a subset of it. So the\n148\tcost of `τ` is `outNarrowness τ`, and\n149\t\n150\t    min_τ cost(τ) = outNarrownessGraph = narrowness = pw(G) + 1\n151\t\n152\t(`outNarrownessGraph_eq_narrowness`, `narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, for\n153\tnonempty `C`). By `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`MOSPGraph.lean`), this is\n154\tthe MOSP value. Inactive customers are isolated vertices of the MOSP graph and\n155\tchange neither side.\n156\t\n157\t**So `¬ Sol_k(∅)` means MOSP > k.** This is the only thing an `unsat` answer\n158\tis allowed to mean. Chu & Stuckey state the correspondence in their §2\n159\t(PDF p. 4); the project had measured it 400/400\n160\t(`../reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1). Item 07 turns it into a lemma.\n161\t\n162\t### 1.5 The search tree\n163\t\n164\t`search(S, Q)` is called with a state and a set `Q ⊆ R(S)` of *old moves*\n165\t(`seen`, §2.6). The node does the following, in the order of Py:254–381 and\n166\tC:414–494:\n167\t\n168\t1. Close the free moves. If `S = C`, answer `sat`.\n169\t2. Memo: if `S` is recorded as refuted, answer `false` (§2.7).\n170\t3. `Q ← Q ∩ R(S)`. The **candidates** are `K = R(S) ∖ Q` under old move, and\n171\t   `K = R(S)` otherwise (Py:274–276, C:453–455).\n172\t4. The **cost cut**: the playable candidates are\n173\t   `P = [c ∈ K : cost(S, c) ≤ k]`, in increasing customer index (Py:315–323,\n174\t   C:282–289).\n175\t5. The **dominance filter** turns `P` into a sublist `L` (§2.2–§2.5; Py:325–327\n176\t   via `_apply_dominance`, C:270–358 `dominance_filter`).\n177\t6. Sort `L` by cost, with ties broken by index, or by the degree fan order\n178\t   (Py:329–332, C:360–391). This changes only the order in which children are\n179\t   visited.\n180\t7. For each `c ∈ L` in that order:\n181\t   - recurse on `S·c` with the inherited old moves `Q'(c)` (§2.6);\n182\t   - on `true`, answer `true`;\n183\t   - otherwise add `c` to `seen` for the later siblings (Py:335–373,\n184\t     C:467–489).\n185\t8. Record `S` as refuted, unless the run aborted, and answer `false`\n186\t   (Py:378–379, C:491).\n187\t\n188\tThe cost cut in step 4 is not a rule. It is the definition of playability, and\n189\tit is exact: an unplayable move is not a move of `Sol_k`. Everything else in\n190\tsteps 2, 3 and 5 is a rule, and each needs a soundness argument.\n191\t\n192\t---\n193\t\n194\t## 2. The rules, as the fixed code implements them\n195\t\n196\t### 2.0 What \"sound\" means at a node\n197\t\n198\tFix a node `(S, Q)` with playable candidates `P` and filtered list `L ⊆ P`.\n199\t\n200\t**Node soundness.** Assume every old move is refuted: `¬ Sol_k(S·q)` for every\n201\t`q ∈ Q`. Then `Sol_k(S)` implies that `Sol_k(S·c)` for some `c ∈ L`.\n202\t\n203\tEvery rule below is justified by a **covering** of the same shape: a discarded\n204\tmove `r` is *covered by* `c` when `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·c)`. Coverings compose\n205\ttransitively. So node soundness follows once the coverings the rules use\n206\tsatisfy one condition: **every chain of coverings from a discarded playable\n207\tcandidate ends in `L` or in `Q`.** That is exactly what the certificate\n208\tchecker's step 5 enforces (Cert:641–657), and a cycle violates it.\n209\t\n210\tA rule's premise can be true of every pair it cites while its composition is\n211\tstill unsound. That is the 2026-09-26 bug.\n212\t\n213\tUnplayable customers need nothing. Customers in `Q` are covered by the\n214\thypothesis.\n215\t\n216\t### 2.1 The free move\n217\t\n218\t**Premise.** `c ∈ C ∖ T` with `N[c] ⊆ O(T)`, i.e. `c ∈ fin(O(T))`, and\n219\t`|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`.\n220\t\n221\t**Conclusion.** No completion is lost by closing every such `c` immediately:\n222\t`P_k(T) ⇔ Sol_k(cl(T))` (Lemma F).\n223\t\n224\t**Code.**\n225\t\n226\t```python\n227\t# Py:246-251\n228\tbits = full & ~closed\n229\twhile bits:\n230\t    ...\n231\t    if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n232\t        found |= bit\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\t```c\n236\t/* C:434-436 */\n237\tmask_t own = s->neighbour[c] & ~opened;\n238\tint size = popcount128(own);\n239\tif (!size) { free_now |= bit; continue; }\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\t**Order.** Free moves are closed first, on entry to every node, before the\n243\tterminal test and the memo. Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule for it: it is\n244\tthe `open = 0` case of Theorem 1 (Py:24–29), with `close ≥ 1 ≥ 0`. The code\n245\tmakes it unconditional, and every quantity below is computed at the free-closed\n246\tstate. This is the modelling choice behind Bug A of `better_move_bug.md` §7.\n247\t\n248\t### 2.2 The definite move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 1)\n249\t\n250\t**Paper.** \"Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if\n251\tU′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (PDF p. 6).\n252\t\n253\t**Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`:\n254\t\n255\t- `q ∈ P`: `q` is a candidate, not in `Q` under old move, and\n256\t  `cost(S, q) ≤ k`;\n257\t- `close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S)`, where `close` counts\n258\t  `d ∈ R(S)` (including `q` itself, and including customers in `Q`) with\n259\t  `o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)`.\n260\t\n261\t**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S) ⇒ Sol_k(S·q)`: some optimal completion from `S`\n262\tbegins with `q`. So `q` covers every other playable candidate.\n263\t\n264\t**Action.** `L = [q]`, for the **first** such `q` in index order. The filter\n265\tstops there: neither the subset rule nor the better move runs at this node.\n266\t\n267\t```python\n268\t# Py:404-412\n269\tif definite_move:\n270\t    for cost, q in playable:\n271\t        own = opens[q]\n272\t        opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n273\t        closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n274\t        if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n275\t            return [(cost, q)]\n276\t```\n277\t\n278\t```c\n279\t/* C:313-330 */\n280\tfor (int j = 0; j < n_remaining; j++)\n281\t    if (sizes[j] <= opened_by && (opens[j] & ~own) == 0) closed_by++;\n282\tif (closed_by >= opened_by) {\n283\t    costs[0] = costs[i]; who[0] = c;\n284\t    count = 1;\n285\t    goto sorted;\n286\t}\n287\t```\n288\t\n289\t`opens` (Py) and `ids`/`opens` (C) range over `R(S)` after the free moves. So\n290\tno finished customer is counted, and customers in `Q` are counted. `close` is a\n291\tproperty of the state and does not depend on the candidate set.\n292\t\n293\t**Acyclic by construction.** One candidate is kept and every other one is\n294\tcovered by it directly.\n295\t\n296\t**Checker.** Step `[\"definite\", q]`. Cert:602–616 recomputes that `q` is\n297\tplayable and that `close ≥ open` over `R(S)`, and makes `q` the cover of every\n298\tother playable candidate. It does not check that `q` is the first in index\n299\torder, which soundness does not need.\n300\t\n301\t### 2.3 The subset rule (Chu & Stuckey §2, with an index tie-break)\n302\t\n303\t**Paper.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can always play\n304\ti before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will close i in\n305\tany case. Hence move j can be removed\" (PDF p. 5).\n306\t\n307\t**Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`, for a playable candidate\n308\t`r ∈ P`: some `d ∈ R(S)` with `d ≠ r` satisfies\n309\t\n310\t    o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S)   and   (o(d, S) ≠ o(r, S)  or  d < r).\n311\t\n312\tThe dominator `d` ranges over **all remaining customers**, not just the\n313\tcandidates. In particular it can be a customer in `Q`. It is never an\n314\tunplayable customer, because `o(d) ⊆ o(r)` gives `cost(S, d) ≤ cost(S, r) ≤ k`.\n315\t\n316\t**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·d)`. Closing `r` makes `d` free\n317\t(`N[d] ⊆ O(S) ∪ o(r, S) = O(S ∪ {r})`), and `d` then `r` reaches\n318\tthe same state as `r` alone, at no greater cost. The inclusion alone gives the\n319\tcovering. The tie-break is there for acyclicity only.\n320\t\n321\t**Action.** Every `r ∈ P` with such a `d` is dropped, and the list keeps its\n322\tindex order. **Fallback:** if every candidate would be dropped, `P` is kept\n323\twhole. Under old move this can happen only when every chain ends in `Q`\n324\t(Py:426–428, C:162–164). The fallback discards nothing, so it is trivially\n325\tsound.\n326\t\n327\t```python\n328\t# Py:417-428\n329\tfor cost, q in playable:\n330\t    own = opens[q]\n331\t    dominated = any(\n332\t        other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n333\t        for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n334\t    )\n335\t    if not dominated:\n336\t        kept.append((cost, q))\n337\treturn kept or playable\n338\t```\n339\t\n340\t```c\n341\t/* C:151-158, subset_pass with exclude = 0 */\n342\tfor (int j = 0; j < n_remaining && !dominated; j++) {\n343\t    if (sizes[j] > own_size) continue;\n344\t    int d = ids[j];\n345\t    if (d == c) continue;\n346\t    if (exclude && ((exclude >> d) & 1)) continue;   /* 0 in today's rule */\n347\t    mask_t other = opens[j];\n348\t    if ((other & ~own) == 0 && (other != own || d < c)) dominated = 1;\n349\t}\n350\t```\n351\t\n352\t**Acyclic by construction.** \"`d` dominates `r`\" means `o(d) ⊊ o(r)`, or\n353\t`o(d) = o(r)` with `d < r`. This is a strict partial order on `R(S)`. So every\n354\tchain ends at a minimal element `m`. That `m` is undominated in `R(S)`, and it\n355\tis either a candidate, which then survives the subset pass, or a member of `Q`.\n356\t\n357\t**Order.** The subset rule runs after the definite move (only when the definite\n358\tmove did not fire) and **before** the better move. Its dominators are measured\n359\tagainst `R(S)`, and nothing has been discarded from `R(S)` when it runs. That\n360\tordering is the 2026-09-26 fix (§2.4).\n361\t\n362\t**Checker.** Step `[\"subset\", r, d]`. Cert:617–627 checks that `r` is playable,\n363\tthat `d ∈ R(S)` with `d ≠ r`, and that the tie-broken inclusion holds. It also\n364\trejects a candidate that is discarded twice.\n365\t\n366\t### 2.4 The better move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 2, corrected), C only\n367\t\n368\t**Paper.** \"Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S ∪ {r})\n369\t≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\n370\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (PDF p. 6). It \"prunes only one branch at a time\"\n371\t(PDF p. 6). `better_move_bug.md` §3a argues that this rule is a *pairwise*\n372\tconditional. It licenses discarding `r` only while `q` is kept.\n373\t\n374\t**Where it runs.** Only in the C (`better_move_pass`, C:169–266), only when the\n375\t`better_move` flag is on, and only when the filtered list after the subset rule\n376\thas at least two entries. The Python reference has no such rule. Cert ports the\n377\tC (`_better_pass`, `_better_premise`, Cert:440–489), and the port agrees with\n378\tthe C in node counts (Cert docstring).\n379\t\n380\t**Input.** `W = [w₀, w₁, …]`, the **survivors of the subset rule**, in\n381\tincreasing index order. The list is not yet sorted by cost; the sort happens\n382\tafter the filter, at C:360.\n383\t\n384\t**Premise.** Let `S_r = S ∪ {r}` and `X_r = O(S) ∪ N[r]`. The code uses `S_r`\n385\titself, not `cl(S_r)`, but `O(cl(S_r)) = X_r` either way. Then `r = w_i` is\n386\tdropped if some `q = w_j` satisfies all of the following:\n387\t\n388\t1. **Earlier and among the first `L`.** `j < i` and `j < L`, where\n389\t   `L = better_move_dominators` (default 4 in `decide`; `0` means every earlier\n390\t   survivor). `csearch` and `recertify` pass `0`\n391\t   (`benchmarks/csearch.py:101`, `benchmarks/recertify.py:70–71`). The C\n392\t   comment at C:178 says \"only the cheapest few q\"; the loop in fact takes the\n393\t   first few *by index*. That is a documentation inaccuracy with no bearing on\n394\t   soundness.\n395\t2. **`S ++ [q]` playable.** `q ∈ W ⊆ P`.\n396\t3. **`S ++ [r, q]` playable in the paper's measure.**\n397\t   `|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ S_r| ≤ k`. The free moves `r` would trigger are\n398\t   deliberately *not* subtracted.\n399\t4. **Corrected close count.** `close′ ≥ open′`, where\n400\t   - `open′ = |N[q] ∖ X_r|`;\n401\t   - `close′ = |{d ∈ C ∖ (S_r) : ∅ ≠ N[d] ∖ X_r ⊆ N[q] ∖ X_r}|`.\n402\t\n403\t   `S` here is the free-closed set of the node. The count leaves out every\n404\t   customer that `r` finishes on its own.\n405\t\n406\t`r = w₀` is never dropped. The dominator `q` may itself be dropped by an\n407\tearlier `q′`: the loop does not consult `survives[qi]`.\n408\t\n409\t**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·q)`.\n410\t\n411\t*Why (for item 10).* Premise 4 is the definite-move premise (§2.2) for `q` at\n412\tthe child state `cl(S_r)`. The remaining set of that state is exactly\n413\t`C ∖ S_r` minus the `d` with `N[d] ⊆ X_r`. Premise 3 bounds `q`'s cost at that\n414\tstate from above, so §2.2 applies there and gives `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(cl(S_r)·q)`.\n415\tIf instead `r` finishes `q` on its own (`N[q] ⊆ X_r`), then `q` is already\n416\tclosed in `cl(S_r)`, and the swap below needs only premises 2 and 3.\n417\t\n418\tThe paper's swap then plays `q` before `r`. The cost of `q` first is at most\n419\t`k` by premise 2. The cost of `r` second is\n420\t`|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ fin(O(S) ∪ N[q])|`. Since `r, q ∈ X_r ∪ N[q]`, this is at most\n421\t`|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ S_r|`, which premise 3 bounds by `k`. Both orders reach the\n422\tstate `cl(S ∪ {q, r})`.\n423\t\n424\t`better_move_bug.md` §7 measures the alternatives:\n425\t\n426\t- the uncorrected count (Bug A) fails on 57 of 126 M applications;\n427\t- \"exact\" playability (subtracting `r`'s free moves) fails on 726 of 125 M;\n428\t- the corrected count with paper playability fails on none of 124 M.\n429\t\n430\t```c\n431\t/* C:213-251 */\n432\tfor (int qi = 0; qi < limit && qi < ri && !pruned; qi++) {\n433\t    int q = who[qi];\n434\t    if (q == r) continue;\n435\t    if (popcount128((opened_r | s->neighbour[q]) & ~closed_r) > s->k)\n436\t        continue;                                   /* premise 3 */\n437\t    mask_t own = s->neighbour[q] & ~opened_r;\n438\t    int opened_by = popcount128(own);\n439\t    int closed_by = 0;\n440\t    for (mask_t bits = remaining_r; bits; ) {\n441\t        mask_t bit = LOWEST(bits); bits ^= bit;\n442\t        mask_t left = s->neighbour[lowest_index(bit)] & ~opened_r;\n443\t        if ((left || count_finished) && (left & ~own) == 0) closed_by++;   /* premise 4 */\n444\t    }\n445\t    if (closed_by >= opened_by) pruned = 1;\n446\t}\n447\t```\n448\t\n449\t`count_finished` is `BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT` (C:195). It is `0` in today's rule and\n450\texists only to measure the revert.\n451\t\n452\t**Acyclic by construction.** Every covering points to a strictly earlier index,\n453\tso `w₀` survives and every chain ends at a survivor.\n454\t\n455\t**The composition, and the two bad forms.** Today's order is\n456\t`definite_move → subset_rule → better_move` (C:353–358):\n457\t\n458\t```c\n459\t} else {\n460\t    if (s->subset_rule)\n461\t        count = subset_pass(s, ids, opens, sizes, n_remaining, 0, costs, who, index_of, count);\n462\t    if (s->better_move)\n463\t        count = better_move_pass(s, closed, opened, costs, who, index_of, count, NULL);\n464\t}\n465\t```\n466\t\n467\tUnder this order a chain of coverings is a run of subset links followed by a\n468\trun of better links, never the other way round. The reason is that the subset\n469\trule has finished before any better-move link exists. A better link's target is\n470\ta subset survivor, so the next link, if there is one, is again a better link,\n471\tto a strictly earlier index. Every chain therefore ends at a better-move\n472\tsurvivor, which is in `L`, or at a customer in `Q`. The C comment at C:293–312\n473\tgives the same argument.\n474\t\n475\tThe two known bad forms are:\n476\t\n477\t- **Bug A, the close count** (`BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT`). Premise 4 also counts the\n478\t  `d` with `N[d] ⊆ X_r`. The rule's own premise is then too weak, and the\n479\t  conclusion of the rule fails on its own.\n480\t- **Bug B, the cross-rule cycle** (`BM_OLD_RULE_ORDER`, C:348–352). The better\n481\t  move runs first. The subset rule then measures its survivors against every\n482\t  customer in `R(S)`, including those the better move discarded. So `r` can go\n483\t  citing `q`, and then `q` can go citing `r`: every premise is true, and the\n484\t  chain has no end.\n485\t\n486\tBug C (the early exit at C:290 ignored `better_move`) was a flag bug. It did\n487\tnot make the rule unsound; it made a soundness control vacuous. A third,\n488\tmeasured-only composition, `BM_SUBSET_RESTRICTED` (C:337–347), runs the better\n489\tmove first and then the subset rule, excluding the better move's discards. It\n490\tis argued sound in the C comment and is not claimed here.\n491\t\n492\t**Checker.** Step `[\"better\", r, q]`. Cert:628–637 checks that `r` and `q` are\n493\tplayable and recomputes `_better_premise` (premises 3 and 4). It does not check\n494\tpremise 1 (the earlier index or `L`); step 5's cycle check stands in for it.\n495\t`_better_premise` is shared with the emitter, so for this rule the checker and\n496\tthe emitter are not independent in the premise's *formula*. They are\n497\tindependent in its evaluation on the certificate's states.\n498\t\n499\t### 2.5 The filter as a whole\n500\t\n501\tAt a node where the definite move does not fire, `L` is the list of better-move\n502\tsurvivors of the subset survivors of `P`. It is `P` itself when the subset\n503\tfallback triggers and the better move is off. When the definite move fires,\n504\t`L = [q]`. The rules are **not** applied to a fixpoint: each runs once, over\n505\tthe list the previous one left.\n506\t\n507\t### 2.6 The old move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 3)\n508\t\n509\t**Paper.** \"Suppose that S′ = [s₁, …, s_m, q, s_{m+1}, …, s_n] is playable, then\n510\tif U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is a solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (PDF p. 7).\n511\tIt adds: \"if … at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and U is\n512\tplayable, then q can immediately be pruned\". The paper also maintains the set\n513\t`Q(S)` incrementally (PDF p. 7).\n514\t\n515\t**What the code maintains.** `Q` is the `seen` argument.\n516\t\n517\t- At the root, `Q = ∅`.\n518\t- At a node `(S, Q)`, a candidate `c` whose subtree has returned `false`\n519\t  without aborting is added to `seen` (Py:373, C:488).\n520\t- A later sibling `c′` passes to its child\n521\t\n522\t      Q′(c′) = {q ∈ seen : |(O(S) ∪ N[q] ∪ N[c′]) ∖ (S ∪ {q})| ≤ k}\n523\t\n524\t  (Py:350–363, C:401–412, `inherit_old_moves`). That is `cost(S ∪ {q}, c′)`:\n525\t  the step `c′` is playable with `q` reinserted before it.\n526\t- On entry, the child intersects with its remaining set (Py:275, C:454).\n527\t\n528\t**Invariant (for item 11).** Suppose `q ∈ Q(S)` at a node `S` on the current\n529\tpath. Then there is an ancestor `A` on that path at which `q` was a searched\n530\tsibling whose subtree has **completed** with `false`. Write the moves from `A`\n531\tto `S` as `a₁, …, a_t`. Then the reinserted path, which starts at `cl(A ∪ {q})`\n532\tand closes `a₁, …, a_t` in turn, is playable, with each `a_j` skipped if `q`\n533\thas already made it free. It ends at `S·q = cl(S ∪ {q})`.\n534\t\n535\t*Why.* Take the step through `a_j`, say from `T` to `T′`. Its opened set with\n536\t`q` inserted is `O(T) ∪ N[q]`, and its closed set contains `T ∪ {q}`. So its\n537\tcost is at most the quantity the inheritance test bounds. The paper's remark\n538\tthat the playability condition \"is in fact crucial\" is this test.\n539\t\n540\t**Premise at the node.** `q ∈ Q(S)`.\n541\t\n542\t**Conclusion.** `¬ Sol_k(S·q)`, as long as the refutation of `cl(A ∪ {q})` is\n543\tgenuine. By the invariant, `Sol_k(S·q) ⇒ Sol_k(cl(A ∪ {q}))`.\n544\t\n545\t**Action.** `q` is removed from the candidates before the cost cut, and it may\n546\tend a covering chain (§2.0).\n547\t\n548\t**Not implemented.** The paper's \"synergy\" (PDF p. 7): if `q ∈ Q(S)` is better\n549\tthan `r`, add `r` to `Q(S)`. Neither implementation does this.\n550\t\n551\t**Checker.** Old move records no steps. Cert re-derives `Q` from the path with\n552\tthe same inheritance test (Cert:659–669) and lets chains end in it\n553\t(Cert:648–649).\n554\t\n555\t### 2.7 The memo (nogood recording)\n556\t\n557\t**Premise.** The closed set `S` of the current node (after free moves) was\n558\trecorded as refuted. Recording happens only when a node's loop finishes\n559\twithout `true` and without an abort (Py:375–379, C:491; `memo_add` also stops\n560\tpast `memo_limit` or 70% load, C:96–97). That only stores fewer entries, which\n561\tis sound.\n562\t\n563\t**Conclusion.** `¬ Sol_k(S)`. Answer `false` without searching.\n564\t\n565\t**Why a key on `S` alone suffices.** By §1.3, `O(S)` is a function of `S`, so\n566\t`Sol_k(S)` is a property of `S`, not of the path that reached it. The memo is\n567\tchecked after the free moves and before anything else (Py:268–272,\n568\tC:450–451).\n569\t\n570\t**Old move and the memo together.** The implementations disagree:\n571\t\n572\t- The **Python refuses** the combination and drops the memo when both are\n573\t  asked for (Py:217–222). Its stated reason (Py:48–55;\n574\t  `../reports/customer_search.md` §3) is that \"a failure reached with old-move\n575\t  pruning depends on which branches an ancestor had searched … and recording\n576\t  it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path would not\".\n577\t- The **C runs both**, as Chu & Stuckey do (PDF p. 12: \"better move\", \"old move\"\n578\t  and nogood recording on together). **This is the configuration of every\n579\t  production refutation**, because `decide`'s defaults are `old_move=True,\n580\t  memo=True` and they go to the C (Py:127–128, Py:199–215). That covers\n581\t  `csearch`, `recertify` and the harness's `default`.\n582\t- **Cert refuses to check** a certificate in that configuration (Cert:526–528).\n583\t\n584\tThe argument of §2.0 and §2.6 says the combination is **sound**, and the\n585\tPython's stated reason does not hold as a soundness argument. The argument is\n586\tan induction on the order in which subtrees complete:\n587\t\n588\t- every refutation completed so far is genuine;\n589\t- a node's `Q` entries rest only on refutations completed before the node was\n590\t  entered (§2.6);\n591\t- so each `Q` entry is genuinely refuted, `¬ Sol_k(S·q)`, and not merely\n592\t  refuted relative to the path;\n593\t- then a refutation of `S` reached with old-move pruning is a genuine\n594\t  `¬ Sol_k(S)`, and recording it against `S` is correct.\n595\t\n596\tWhat *is* path-dependent is **local checkability**, which is Cert's point: to\n597\tcheck a memo reference under old move, a checker must re-derive the cited\n598\tsubtree's `Q`, or trust the global induction. That is a property of the\n599\tcertificate format. It does not make the search unsound.\n600\t\n601\tThe empirical record agrees with the argument:\n602\t\n603\t- 10,476 exhaustive C decisions with both on agree with brute force (Py:218);\n604\t- the differential harness runs both configurations through the C with both\n605\t  on, and recorded 0 disagreements on 878,580 + 878,700 calls at 9–40\n606\t  customers (`better_move_bug.md` §7) and on 25,800 calls at 50–100\n607\t  (`../reports/ml_nature.md` §33).\n608\t\n609\tItem 11 is to prove this or find the counterexample. Until then it is a\n610\t**claim, not a finding**.\n611\t\n612\t### 2.8 Out of scope\n613\t\n614\t- **`restrict=True`** (their `ub_MOSP`, Py:277–282, C:456–459) branches only on\n615\t  already-open customers. It is unsound by design, and it never answers `unsat`\n616\t  (Py:386–387, `native.py:211`).\n617\t- **`expansion_prune`** (Py:293–313) is a whole-node cut that exists only in\n618\t  the Python and is off by default. It is not claimed here.\n619\t- **`branch` and `fan_order`** only reorder `L` (Py:329–334) and cannot change\n620\t  the answer.\n621\t- **`max_nodes` and `deadline`** produce `unknown`, never `unsat`, and they\n622\t  suppress memo recording (Py:337–345, Py:378).\n623\t\n624\t### 2.9 The composition claimed sound\n625\t\n626\t**Claim (to be proved as item 12's theorem).** Fix an instance and `k ≥ 0`.\n627\tRun `decide` with `restrict=False`, `expansion_prune=False`, `branch=None`,\n628\t`better_move_variant = 0`, any fan order, and any setting of the following:\n629\t\n630\t- `subset_rule ∈ {on, off}`;\n631\t- `definite_move ∈ {on, off}`;\n632\t- `better_move ∈ {on, off}` (C), with any `better_move_dominators = L ≥ 0`;\n633\t- `old_move ∈ {on, off}`;\n634\t- `memo ∈ {on, off}`, with the rules applied in the order of §1.5 and §2.5:\n635\t  free moves, memo, the old-move candidate set, the cost cut, and then\n636\t  `definite_move → subset_rule → better_move`, each once, each citing only\n637\t  customers still standing or in `Q`.\n638\t\n639\tIf `decide` answers `unsat`, then `¬ Sol_k(∅)`, so MOSP > k and pw(G) > k − 1.\n640\t\n641\tThe configurations that produced the corpus's refutations are all instances of\n642\tthe claim:\n643\t\n644\t| name | rules | where |\n645\t|---|---|---|\n646\t| Python reference | free, definite, subset, old move (memo dropped) | `decide(native=False)` |\n647\t| C default | free, definite, subset, old move, **memo** | `decide()`; harness `default` |\n648\t| `csearch` | the C default plus better move with `L = 0` when ≤ 5 products per customer | `benchmarks/csearch.py:101` |\n649\t| `recertify` | the C default plus better move with `L = 0`, always | `benchmarks/recertify.py:70–71` |\n650\t| Cert `memo` | free, definite, subset, memo (no old move) | `learning/search_certificate.py` |\n651\t\n652\tThe claim covers the old-move-plus-memo combination (§2.7) and is **false** for\n653\tthe variant bits `BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT` and `BM_OLD_RULE_ORDER` (§2.4). Item 06\n654\tis to confirm both of those failures by brute force, and item 10 is to prove a\n655\tLean counterexample to each.\n656\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fefaae11-7851-456f-8e91-99cdb5c832da","uuid":"84f0a471-4411-43af-bc17-c4b36859cdec","timestamp":"2026-09-30T21:44:35.529Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/fefaae11-7851-456f-8e91-99cdb5c832da/tool-results/b3j0rhhb0.txt","content":"# Soundness of the customer search\n\nSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4). Every certified\nrefutation in the corpus rests on one claim: that the pruning rules of Chu &\nStuckey's (2009) customer search never discard the last solution. Two\n`better_move` bugs broke that claim in code, and testing caught both\n(`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §1–§3 and §7). This document states the claim\nprecisely and then proves it.\n\n- §1 models the search as a mathematical object.\n- §2 states each rule *as the fixed code implements it*, quotes the code lines,\n  and says which composition of rules is claimed sound.\n- §3 (item 06) checks every statement by brute force.\n- §4 (items 07–11) gives the Lean proofs.\n- §5 (item 12) states the theorem and maps each premise the certificate checker\n  verifies to the Lean lemma that justifies it.\n\n§1–2 were written in loop0006 item 05 and contain no Lean.\n\n**Code references** are to the tree at commit `8d824d034`:\n`satisfiability/customer_search.py` (**Py**, the reference implementation),\n`satisfiability/customer_search.c` (**C**), and\n`learning/search_certificate.py` (**Cert**, the emitter and the independent\nchecker). Line numbers are given as `Py:238–252`. Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009)\nare those of `literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`, the preprint, cited as \"PDF p.\".\n\n**Status of the statements.** Every conclusion in §2 was spot-checked before\nit was written down, using a throwaway script (not item 06's deliverable).\nThe script computes the predicate `Sol` of §1.4 exactly by dynamic programming\nover closed sets, on 300 random graphs with 2–8 vertices at every `k`, 1,461\n`(graph, k)` pairs in all. The checks were:\n\n- Lemma F (free moves);\n- the conclusion of the definite move at every playable `q` meeting its\n  premise, in every state `cl(T)` with `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`;\n- the conclusion of the subset rule for every ordered pair;\n- the corrected better move for every ordered pair.\n\nThere were zero failures. This is evidence, not proof: the statements are\nclaims until items 06–12 settle them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The search as a mathematical object\n\n### 1.1 Instance, customers, neighbourhoods\n\nAn instance is a 0/1 matrix `M` with rows as customers and columns as products.\nThe search sees only the **active** customers `C`, those with at least one\nproduct (`Py:225`). A customer with no product never opens a stack, and the\nsearch leaves it out of the witness (`Py:193–194`). On `C`, `N[c]` is the\nself-inclusive neighbourhood in the MOSP graph: `d ∈ N[c]` iff `d = c` or some\nproduct is needed by both (`Py:224`, `_neighbour_masks`; Cert has its own\n`neighbourhoods`, which shares no code with the search).\n\nChu & Stuckey observe (2009, §2, PDF p. 4) that the products matter only through\nthis graph: \"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the\nsame minimum number of stacks\". So everything below is a statement about a\nfinite graph `G` on `C` and an integer `k ≥ 0`.\n\n### 1.2 Opened sets and the quantities the rules use\n\nFor `T ⊆ C`:\n\n| symbol | definition | code |\n|---|---|---|\n| `O(T)` | `⋃_{c ∈ T} N[c]`, the stacks *opened* so far (`O(∅) = ∅`) | `opened` |\n| `fin(X)` | `{c ∈ C : N[c] ⊆ X}`, the customers finished once `X` is open | `free_moves`, Py:238–252 |\n| `o(c, T)` | `N[c] ∖ O(T)`, what closing `c` next would newly open | `opens[c]`, Py:285–291; C:431–441 |\n| `open(c, T)` | `|o(c, T)|` | `own.bit_count()`, `sizes[j]` |\n| `close(c, T)` | `|{d ∈ C ∖ fin(O(T)) : o(d, T) ⊆ o(c, T)}|` | Py:408, C:320–323 |\n\nTwo facts are used everywhere, and both are immediate:\n\n- `T ⊆ fin(O(T))`;\n- `O(fin(O(T))) = O(T)`. Every `N[c]` with `c ∈ fin(O(T))` lies inside `O(T)`,\n  and `T ⊆ fin(O(T))` gives the reverse inclusion.\n\n`close` is Chu & Stuckey's count (PDF p. 4), \"the number of new stacks that will\nclose\", with one difference. The paper's `d` ranges over every customer, while\nthe code's ranges only over customers not yet finished, the `d` with\n`o(d, T) ≠ ∅`. The code closes every finished customer before any rule runs\n(§1.3), so at the states where the rules run the two ranges differ only by\ncustomers already closed. `close(c, T)` counts `c` itself whenever\n`o(c, T) ≠ ∅`.\n\n### 1.3 Moves, costs, and free-closed states\n\n**A move** closes one customer. At closed set `T`, closing `c ∉ T` costs\n\n    cost(T, c) = |O(T ∪ {c}) ∖ T| = |O(T) ∖ T| + open(c, T),\n\nthe stacks open at the moment `c` closes, counting `c` itself (Py:17–22,\nPy:321; C:275–287 computes it in the second form, as `open_now + sizes[j]`).\nThis is the paper's `|O(S) − S ∪ o(c, S)|` (PDF p. 4). The move is **playable**\nwhen `cost(T, c) ≤ k`. A **closing order from `T`** is an enumeration\n`c₁, …, c_m` of `C ∖ T`, and its cost is `max_i cost(T ∪ {c₁, …, c_{i−1}}, c_i)`\n(0 when `m = 0`).\n\n**The plain predicate.** `P_k(T)` means that some closing order from `T` has\ncost at most `k`.\n\n**Free-closed states.** The search never stops at an arbitrary `T`. On entering\na node it closes every remaining customer whose neighbourhood is already open\n(the *free moves*, Py:259–266 and C:431–448). A free move opens nothing, and\nopening nothing creates no new free moves, so one pass suffices (Py:241–243).\nThe node's closed set is therefore\n\n    cl(T) = fin(O(T)),\n\nand by §1.2 the opened set `O(cl(T)) = O(T)` is a function of the closed set.\nThis is why the memo can key on the closed set alone (Py:148–150, C:451). A\n**state** is a free-closed set `S = cl(S)`. Its **remaining** customers are\n`R(S) = C ∖ S`, and each has `o(c, S) ≠ ∅`. The **child** of `S` by `c ∈ R(S)`\nis `S·c = cl(S ∪ {c})`. The search calls itself on `closed | bit(c)`, and the\ncallee closes the free moves (Py:364, C:482).\n\n**The search's predicate.** For a state `S`, `Sol_k(S)` holds iff `S = C`, or\nsome `c ∈ R(S)` has `cost(S, c) ≤ k` and `Sol_k(S·c)`.\n\nThe root is `∅ = cl(∅)`, because every `N[c]` is nonempty.\n\n### 1.4 The decision question\n\nThe search answers the question: **is there a closing order of cost ≤ k from\n∅?** When the cost cut is its only pruning, it returns `sat` exactly when\n`Sol_k(∅)`. The whole point of §2 is to show that the rules do not change this\nanswer.\n\nTwo facts connect this question to MOSP. Both are for items 07 and 12 to prove\nin Lean.\n\n**Lemma F (free moves).** Suppose `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`. Then\n`P_k(T) ⇔ Sol_k(cl(T))`.\n\n*Why.* (⇒) In any closing order from `T`, a customer `f ∈ fin(O(T)) ∖ T` can be\nmoved to the front. That changes no opened set, and every step before `f`'s old\nposition gets cheaper by one, because `f ∈ O(T)` was counted as open there.\n(⇐) Closing the free customers one at a time costs at most `|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`\neach. The hypothesis holds at the root (`0`) and at every child, where it is\nthe parent's step cost minus one. So it holds at every node the search visits.\n\n**Cost = out-narrowness = pathwidth + 1.** Take a full closing order\n`τ = c₁, …, c_n` from `∅`, and write `S_i = {c₁, …, c_i}`. Its `i`-th step costs\n`|N[S_i] ∖ S_{i−1}|`. That is `outShackBeforeMove τ (i−1)` of Kornai & Tuza's\ndual shack process, formalised in `Complex/Narrowness.lean` (`EnteredBy`,\n`outShackBeforeMove`). The shack just after the move is a subset of it. So the\ncost of `τ` is `outNarrowness τ`, and\n\n    min_τ cost(τ) = outNarrownessGraph = narrowness = pw(G) + 1\n\n(`outNarrownessGraph_eq_narrowness`, `narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one`, for\nnonempty `C`). By `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one` (`MOSPGraph.lean`), this is\nthe MOSP value. Inactive customers are isolated vertices of the MOSP graph and\nchange neither side.\n\n**So `¬ Sol_k(∅)` means MOSP > k.** This is the only thing an `unsat` answer\nis allowed to mean. Chu & Stuckey state the correspondence in their §2\n(PDF p. 4); the project had measured it 400/400\n(`../reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1). Item 07 turns it into a lemma.\n\n### 1.5 The search tree\n\n`search(S, Q)` is called with a state and a set `Q ⊆ R(S)` of *old moves*\n(`seen`, §2.6). The node does the following, in the order of Py:254–381 and\nC:414–494:\n\n1. Close the free moves. If `S = C`, answer `sat`.\n2. Memo: if `S` is recorded as refuted, answer `false` (§2.7).\n3. `Q ← Q ∩ R(S)`. The **candidates** are `K = R(S) ∖ Q` under old move, and\n   `K = R(S)` otherwise (Py:274–276, C:453–455).\n4. The **cost cut**: the playable candidates are\n   `P = [c ∈ K : cost(S, c) ≤ k]`, in increasing customer index (Py:315–323,\n   C:282–289).\n5. The **dominance filter** turns `P` into a sublist `L` (§2.2–§2.5; Py:325–327\n   via `_apply_dominance`, C:270–358 `dominance_filter`).\n6. Sort `L` by cost, with ties broken by index, or by the degree fan order\n   (Py:329–332, C:360–391). This changes only the order in which children are\n   visited.\n7. For each `c ∈ L` in that order:\n   - recurse on `S·c` with the inherited old moves `Q'(c)` (§2.6);\n   - on `true`, answer `true`;\n   - otherwise add `c` to `seen` for the later siblings (Py:335–373,\n     C:467–489).\n8. Record `S` as refuted, unless the run aborted, and answer `false`\n   (Py:378–379, C:491).\n\nThe cost cut in step 4 is not a rule. It is the definition of playability, and\nit is exact: an unplayable move is not a move of `Sol_k`. Everything else in\nsteps 2, 3 and 5 is a rule, and each needs a soundness argument.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The rules, as the fixed code implements them\n\n### 2.0 What \"sound\" means at a node\n\nFix a node `(S, Q)` with playable candidates `P` and filtered list `L ⊆ P`.\n\n**Node soundness.** Assume every old move is refuted: `¬ Sol_k(S·q)` for every\n`q ∈ Q`. Then `Sol_k(S)` implies that `Sol_k(S·c)` for some `c ∈ L`.\n\nEvery rule below is justified by a **covering** of the same shape: a discarded\nmove `r` is *covered by* `c` when `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·c)`. Coverings compose\ntransitively. So node soundness follows once the coverings the rules use\nsatisfy one condition: **every chain of coverings from a discarded playable\ncandidate ends in `L` or in `Q`.** That is exactly what the certificate\nchecker's step 5 enforces (Cert:641–657), and a cycle violates it.\n\nA rule's premise can be true of every pair it cites while its composition is\nstill unsound. That is the 2026-09-26 bug.\n\nUnplayable customers need nothing. Customers in `Q` are covered by the\nhypothesis.\n\n### 2.1 The free move\n\n**Premise.** `c ∈ C ∖ T` with `N[c] ⊆ O(T)`, i.e. `c ∈ fin(O(T))`, and\n`|O(T) ∖ T| ≤ k`.\n\n**Conclusion.** No completion is lost by closing every such `c` immediately:\n`P_k(T) ⇔ Sol_k(cl(T))` (Lemma F).\n\n**Code.**\n\n```python\n# Py:246-251\nbits = full & ~closed\nwhile bits:\n    ...\n    if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n        found |= bit\n```\n\n```c\n/* C:434-436 */\nmask_t own = s->neighbour[c] & ~opened;\nint size = popcount128(own);\nif (!size) { free_now |= bit; continue; }\n```\n\n**Order.** Free moves are closed first, on entry to every node, before the\nterminal test and the memo. Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule for it: it is\nthe `open = 0` case of Theorem 1 (Py:24–29), with `close ≥ 1 ≥ 0`. The code\nmakes it unconditional, and every quantity below is computed at the free-closed\nstate. This is the modelling choice behind Bug A of `better_move_bug.md` §7.\n\n### 2.2 The definite move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 1)\n\n**Paper.** \"Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if\nU′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (PDF p. 6).\n\n**Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`:\n\n- `q ∈ P`: `q` is a candidate, not in `Q` under old move, and\n  `cost(S, q) ≤ k`;\n- `close(q, S) ≥ open(q, S)`, where `close` counts\n  `d ∈ R(S)` (including `q` itself, and including customers in `Q`) with\n  `o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)`.\n\n**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S) ⇒ Sol_k(S·q)`: some optimal completion from `S`\nbegins with `q`. So `q` covers every other playable candidate.\n\n**Action.** `L = [q]`, for the **first** such `q` in index order. The filter\nstops there: neither the subset rule nor the better move runs at this node.\n\n```python\n# Py:404-412\nif definite_move:\n    for cost, q in playable:\n        own = opens[q]\n        opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n        closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n        if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n            return [(cost, q)]\n```\n\n```c\n/* C:313-330 */\nfor (int j = 0; j < n_remaining; j++)\n    if (sizes[j] <= opened_by && (opens[j] & ~own) == 0) closed_by++;\nif (closed_by >= opened_by) {\n    costs[0] = costs[i]; who[0] = c;\n    count = 1;\n    goto sorted;\n}\n```\n\n`opens` (Py) and `ids`/`opens` (C) range over `R(S)` after the free moves. So\nno finished customer is counted, and customers in `Q` are counted. `close` is a\nproperty of the state and does not depend on the candidate set.\n\n**Acyclic by construction.** One candidate is kept and every other one is\ncovered by it directly.\n\n**Checker.** Step `[\"definite\", q]`. Cert:602–616 recomputes that `q` is\nplayable and that `close ≥ open` over `R(S)`, and makes `q` the cover of every\nother playable candidate. It does not check that `q` is the first in index\norder, which soundness does not need.\n\n### 2.3 The subset rule (Chu & Stuckey §2, with an index tie-break)\n\n**Paper.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can always play\ni before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will close i in\nany case. Hence move j can be removed\" (PDF p. 5).\n\n**Premise, as the code checks it.** At state `S`, for a playable candidate\n`r ∈ P`: some `d ∈ R(S)` with `d ≠ r` satisfies\n\n    o(d, S) ⊆ o(r, S)   and   (o(d, S) ≠ o(r, S)  or  d < r).\n\nThe dominator `d` ranges over **all remaining customers**, not just the\ncandidates. In particular it can be a customer in `Q`. It is never an\nunplayable customer, because `o(d) ⊆ o(r)` gives `cost(S, d) ≤ cost(S, r) ≤ k`.\n\n**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·d)`. Closing `r` makes `d` free\n(`N[d] ⊆ O(S) ∪ o(r, S) = O(S ∪ {r})`), and `d` then `r` reaches\nthe same state as `r` alone, at no greater cost. The inclusion alone gives the\ncovering. The tie-break is there for acyclicity only.\n\n**Action.** Every `r ∈ P` with such a `d` is dropped, and the list keeps its\nindex order. **Fallback:** if every candidate would be dropped, `P` is kept\nwhole. Under old move this can happen only when every chain ends in `Q`\n(Py:426–428, C:162–164). The fallback discards nothing, so it is trivially\nsound.\n\n```python\n# Py:417-428\nfor cost, q in playable:\n    own = opens[q]\n    dominated = any(\n        other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n        for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n    )\n    if not dominated:\n        kept.append((cost, q))\nreturn kept or playable\n```\n\n```c\n/* C:151-158, subset_pass with exclude = 0 */\nfor (int j = 0; j < n_remaining && !dominated; j++) {\n    if (sizes[j] > own_size) continue;\n    int d = ids[j];\n    if (d == c) continue;\n    if (exclude && ((exclude >> d) & 1)) continue;   /* 0 in today's rule */\n    mask_t other = opens[j];\n    if ((other & ~own) == 0 && (other != own || d < c)) dominated = 1;\n}\n```\n\n**Acyclic by construction.** \"`d` dominates `r`\" means `o(d) ⊊ o(r)`, or\n`o(d) = o(r)` with `d < r`. This is a strict partial order on `R(S)`. So every\nchain ends at a minimal element `m`. That `m` is undominated in `R(S)`, and it\nis either a candidate, which then survives the subset pass, or a member of `Q`.\n\n**Order.** The subset rule runs after the definite move (only when the definite\nmove did not fire) and **before** the better move. Its dominators are measured\nagainst `R(S)`, and nothing has been discarded from `R(S)` when it runs. That\nordering is the 2026-09-26 fix (§2.4).\n\n**Checker.** Step `[\"subset\", r, d]`. Cert:617–627 checks that `r` is playable,\nthat `d ∈ R(S)` with `d ≠ r`, and that the tie-broken inclusion holds. It also\nrejects a candidate that is discarded twice.\n\n### 2.4 The better move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 2, corrected), C only\n\n**Paper.** \"Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S ∪ {r})\n≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (PDF p. 6). It \"prunes only one branch at a time\"\n(PDF p. 6). `better_move_bug.md` §3a argues that this rule is a *pairwise*\nconditional. It licenses discarding `r` only while `q` is kept.\n\n**Where it runs.** Only in the C (`better_move_pass`, C:169–266), only when the\n`better_move` flag is on, and only when the filtered list after the subset rule\nhas at least two entries. The Python reference has no such rule. Cert ports the\nC (`_better_pass`, `_better_premise`, Cert:440–489), and the port agrees with\nthe C in node counts (Cert docstring).\n\n**Input.** `W = [w₀, w₁, …]`, the **survivors of the subset rule**, in\nincreasing index order. The list is not yet sorted by cost; the sort happens\nafter the filter, at C:360.\n\n**Premise.** Let `S_r = S ∪ {r}` and `X_r = O(S) ∪ N[r]`. The code uses `S_r`\nitself, not `cl(S_r)`, but `O(cl(S_r)) = X_r` either way. Then `r = w_i` is\ndropped if some `q = w_j` satisfies all of the following:\n\n1. **Earlier and among the first `L`.** `j < i` and `j < L`, where\n   `L = better_move_dominators` (default 4 in `decide`; `0` means every earlier\n   survivor). `csearch` and `recertify` pass `0`\n   (`benchmarks/csearch.py:101`, `benchmarks/recertify.py:70–71`). The C\n   comment at C:178 says \"only the cheapest few q\"; the loop in fact takes the\n   first few *by index*. That is a documentation inaccuracy with no bearing on\n   soundness.\n2. **`S ++ [q]` playable.** `q ∈ W ⊆ P`.\n3. **`S ++ [r, q]` playable in the paper's measure.**\n   `|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ S_r| ≤ k`. The free moves `r` would trigger are\n   deliberately *not* subtracted.\n4. **Corrected close count.** `close′ ≥ open′`, where\n   - `open′ = |N[q] ∖ X_r|`;\n   - `close′ = |{d ∈ C ∖ (S_r) : ∅ ≠ N[d] ∖ X_r ⊆ N[q] ∖ X_r}|`.\n\n   `S` here is the free-closed set of the node. The count leaves out every\n   customer that `r` finishes on its own.\n\n`r = w₀` is never dropped. The dominator `q` may itself be dropped by an\nearlier `q′`: the loop does not consult `survives[qi]`.\n\n**Conclusion.** `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(S·q)`.\n\n*Why (for item 10).* Premise 4 is the definite-move premise (§2.2) for `q` at\nthe child state `cl(S_r)`. The remaining set of that state is exactly\n`C ∖ S_r` minus the `d` with `N[d] ⊆ X_r`. Premise 3 bounds `q`'s cost at that\nstate from above, so §2.2 applies there and gives `Sol_k(S·r) ⇒ Sol_k(cl(S_r)·q)`.\nIf instead `r` finishes `q` on its own (`N[q] ⊆ X_r`), then `q` is already\nclosed in `cl(S_r)`, and the swap below needs only premises 2 and 3.\n\nThe paper's swap then plays `q` before `r`. The cost of `q` first is at most\n`k` by premise 2. The cost of `r` second is\n`|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ fin(O(S) ∪ N[q])|`. Since `r, q ∈ X_r ∪ N[q]`, this is at most\n`|(X_r ∪ N[q]) ∖ S_r|`, which premise 3 bounds by `k`. Both orders reach the\nstate `cl(S ∪ {q, r})`.\n\n`better_move_bug.md` §7 measures the alternatives:\n\n- the uncorrected count (Bug A) fails on 57 of 126 M applications;\n- \"exact\" playability (subtracting `r`'s free moves) fails on 726 of 125 M;\n- the corrected count with paper playability fails on none of 124 M.\n\n```c\n/* C:213-251 */\nfor (int qi = 0; qi < limit && qi < ri && !pruned; qi++) {\n    int q = who[qi];\n    if (q == r) continue;\n    if (popcount128((opened_r | s->neighbour[q]) & ~closed_r) > s->k)\n        continue;                                   /* premise 3 */\n    mask_t own = s->neighbour[q] & ~opened_r;\n    int opened_by = popcount128(own);\n    int closed_by = 0;\n    for (mask_t bits = remaining_r; bits; ) {\n        mask_t bit = LOWEST(bits); bits ^= bit;\n        mask_t left = s->neighbour[lowest_index(bit)] & ~opened_r;\n        if ((left || count_finished) && (left & ~own) == 0) closed_by++;   /* premise 4 */\n    }\n    if (closed_by >= opened_by) pruned = 1;\n}\n```\n\n`count_finished` is `BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT` (C:195). It is `0` in today's rule and\nexists only to measure the revert.\n\n**Acyclic by construction.** Every covering points to a strictly earlier index,\nso `w₀` survives and every chain ends at a survivor.\n\n**The composition, and the two bad forms.** Today's order is\n`definite_move → subset_rule → better_move` (C:353–358):\n\n```c\n} else {\n    if (s->subset_rule)\n        count = subset_pass(s, ids, opens, sizes, n_remaining, 0, costs, who, index_of, count);\n    if (s->better_move)\n        count = better_move_pass(s, closed, opened, costs, who, index_of, count, NULL);\n}\n```\n\nUnder this order a chain of coverings is a run of subset links followed by a\nrun of better links, never the other way round. The reason is that the subset\nrule has finished before any better-move link exists. A better link's target is\na subset survivor, so the next link, if there is one, is again a better link,\nto a strictly earlier index. Every chain therefore ends at a better-move\nsurvivor, which is in `L`, or at a customer in `Q`. The C comment at C:293–312\ngives the same argument.\n\nThe two known bad forms are:\n\n- **Bug A, the close count** (`BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT`). Premise 4 also counts the\n  `d` with `N[d] ⊆ X_r`. The rule's own premise is then too weak, and the\n  conclusion of the rule fails on its own.\n- **Bug B, the cross-rule cycle** (`BM_OLD_RULE_ORDER`, C:348–352). The better\n  move runs first. The subset rule then measures its survivors against every\n  customer in `R(S)`, including those the better move discarded. So `r` can go\n  citing `q`, and then `q` can go citing `r`: every premise is true, and the\n  chain has no end.\n\nBug C (the early exit at C:290 ignored `better_move`) was a flag bug. It did\nnot make the rule unsound; it made a soundness control vacuous. A third,\nmeasured-only composition, `BM_SUBSET_RESTRICTED` (C:337–347), runs the better\nmove first and then the subset rule, excluding the better move's discards. It\nis argued sound in the C comment and is not claimed here.\n\n**Checker.** Step `[\"better\", r, q]`. Cert:628–637 checks that `r` and `q` are\nplayable and recomputes `_better_premise` (premises 3 and 4). It does not check\npremise 1 (the earlier index or `L`); step 5's cycle check stands in for it.\n`_better_premise` is shared with the emitter, so for this rule the checker and\nthe emitter are not independent in the premise's *formula*. They are\nindependent in its evaluation on the certificate's states.\n\n### 2.5 The filter as a whole\n\nAt a node where the definite move does not fire, `L` is the list of better-move\nsurvivors of the subset survivors of `P`. It is `P` itself when the subset\nfallback triggers and the better move is off. When the definite move fires,\n`L = [q]`. The rules are **not** applied to a fixpoint: each runs once, over\nthe list the previous one left.\n\n### 2.6 The old move (Chu & Stuckey Theorem 3)\n\n**Paper.** \"Suppose that S′ = [s₁, …, s_m, q, s_{m+1}, …, s_n] is playable, then\nif U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is a solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (PDF p. 7).\nIt adds: \"if … at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and U is\nplayable, then q can immediately be pruned\". The paper also maintains the set\n`Q(S)` incrementally (PDF p. 7).\n\n**What the code maintains.** `Q` is the `seen` argument.\n\n- At the root, `Q = ∅`.\n- At a node `(S, Q)`, a candidate `c` whose subtree has returned `false`\n  without aborting is added to `seen` (Py:373, C:488).\n- A later sibling `c′` passes to its child\n\n      Q′(c′) = {q ∈ seen : |(O(S) ∪ N[q] ∪ N[c′]) ∖ (S ∪ {q})| ≤ k}\n\n  (Py:350–363, C:401–412, `inherit_old_moves`). That is `cost(S ∪ {q}, c′)`:\n  the step `c′` is playable with `q` reinserted before it.\n- On entry, the child intersects with its remaining set (Py:275, C:454).\n\n**Invariant (for item 11).** Suppose `q ∈ Q(S)` at a node `S` on the current\npath. Then there is an ancestor `A` on that path at which `q` was a searched\nsibling whose subtree has **completed** with `false`. Write the moves from `A`\nto `S` as `a₁, …, a_t`. Then the reinserted path, which starts at `cl(A ∪ {q})`\nand closes `a₁, …, a_t` in turn, is playable, with each `a_j` skipped if `q`\nhas already made it free. It ends at `S·q = cl(S ∪ {q})`.\n\n*Why.* Take the step through `a_j`, say from `T` to `T′`. Its opened set with\n`q` inserted is `O(T) ∪ N[q]`, and its closed set contains `T ∪ {q}`. So its\ncost is at most the quantity the inheritance test bounds. The paper's remark\nthat the playability condition \"is in fact crucial\" is this test.\n\n**Premise at the node.** `q ∈ Q(S)`.\n\n**Conclusion.** `¬ Sol_k(S·q)`, as long as the refutation of `cl(A ∪ {q})` is\ngenuine. By the invariant, `Sol_k(S·q) ⇒ Sol_k(cl(A ∪ {q}))`.\n\n**Action.** `q` is removed from the candidates before the cost cut, and it may\nend a covering chain (§2.0).\n\n**Not implemented.** The paper's \"synergy\" (PDF p. 7): if `q ∈ Q(S)` is better\nthan `r`, add `r` to `Q(S)`. Neither implementation does this.\n\n**Checker.** Old move records no steps. Cert re-derives `Q` from the path with\nthe same inheritance test (Cert:659–669) and lets chains end in it\n(Cert:648–649).\n\n### 2.7 The memo (nogood recording)\n\n**Premise.** The closed set `S` of the current node (after free moves) was\nrecorded as refuted. Recording happens only when a node's loop finishes\nwithout `true` and without an abort (Py:375–379, C:491; `memo_add` also stops\npast `memo_limit` or 70% load, C:96–97). That only stores fewer entries, which\nis sound.\n\n**Conclusion.** `¬ Sol_k(S)`. Answer `false` without searching.\n\n**Why a key on `S` alone suffices.** By §1.3, `O(S)` is a function of `S`, so\n`Sol_k(S)` is a property of `S`, not of the path that reached it. The memo is\nchecked after the free moves and before anything else (Py:268–272,\nC:450–451).\n\n**Old move and the memo together.** The implementations disagree:\n\n- The **Python refuses** the combination and drops the memo when both are\n  asked for (Py:217–222). Its stated reason (Py:48–55;\n  `../reports/customer_search.md` §3) is that \"a failure reached with old-move\n  pruning depends on which branches an ancestor had searched … and recording\n  it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path would not\".\n- The **C runs both**, as Chu & Stuckey do (PDF p. 12: \"better move\", \"old move\"\n  and nogood recording on together). **This is the configuration of every\n  production refutation**, because `decide`'s defaults are `old_move=True,\n  memo=True` and they go to the C (Py:127–128, Py:199–215). That covers\n  `csearch`, `recertify` and the harness's `default`.\n- **Cert refuses to check** a certificate in that configuration (Cert:526–528).\n\nThe argument of §2.0 and §2.6 says the combination is **sound**, and the\nPython's stated reason does not hold as a soundness argument. The argument is\nan induction on the order in which subtrees complete:\n\n- every refutation completed so far is genuine;\n- a node's `Q` entries rest only on refutations completed before the node was\n  entered (§2.6);\n- so each `Q` entry is genuinely refuted, `¬ Sol_k(S·q)`, and not merely\n  refuted relative to the path;\n- then a refutation of `S` reached with old-move pruning is a genuine\n  `¬ Sol_k(S)`, and recording it against `S` is correct.\n\nWhat *is* path-dependent is **local checkability**, which is Cert's point: to\ncheck a memo reference under old move, a checker must re-derive the cited\nsubtree's `Q`, or trust the global induction. That is a property of the\ncertificate format. It does not make the search unsound.\n\nThe empirical record agrees with the argument:\n\n- 10,476 exhaustive C decisions with both on agree with brute force (Py:218);\n- the differential harness runs both configurations through the C with both\n  on, and recorded 0 disagreements on 878,580 + 878,700 calls at 9–40\n  customers (`better_move_bug.md` §7) and on 25,800 calls at 50–100\n  (`../reports/ml_nature.md` §33).\n\nItem 11 is to prove this or find the counterexample. Until then it is a\n**claim, not a finding**.\n\n### 2.8 Out of scope\n\n- **`restrict=True`** (their `ub_MOSP`, Py:277–282, C:456–459) branches only on\n  already-open customers. It is unsound by design, and it never answers `unsat`\n  (Py:386–387, `native.py:211`).\n- **`expansion_prune`** (Py:293–313) is a whole-node cut that exists only in\n  the Python and is off by default. It is not claimed here.\n- **`branch` and `fan_order`** only reorder `L` (Py:329–334) and cannot change\n  the answer.\n- **`max_nodes` and `deadline`** produce `unknown`, never `unsat`, and they\n  suppress memo recording (Py:337–345, Py:378).\n\n### 2.9 The composition claimed sound\n\n**Claim (to be proved as item 12's theorem).** Fix an instance and `k ≥ 0`.\nRun `decide` with `restrict=False`, `expansion_prune=False`, `branch=None`,\n`better_move_variant = 0`, any fan order, and any setting of the following:\n\n- `subset_rule ∈ {on, off}`;\n- `definite_move ∈ {on, off}`;\n- `better_move ∈ {on, off}` (C), with any `better_move_dominators = L ≥ 0`;\n- `old_move ∈ {on, off}`;\n- `memo ∈ {on, off}`, with the rules applied in the order of §1.5 and §2.5:\n  free moves, memo, the old-move candidate set, the cost cut, and then\n  `definite_move → subset_rule → better_move`, each once, each citing only\n  customers still standing or in `Q`.\n\nIf `decide` answers `unsat`, then `¬ Sol_k(∅)`, so MOSP > k and pw(G) > k − 1.\n\nThe configurations that produced the corpus's refutations are all instances of\nthe claim:\n\n| name | rules | where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Python reference | free, definite, subset, old move (memo dropped) | `decide(native=False)` |\n| C default | free, definite, subset, old move, **memo** | `decide()`; harness `default` |\n| `csearch` | the C default plus better move with `L = 0` when ≤ 5 products per customer | `benchmarks/csearch.py:101` |\n| `recertify` | the C default plus better move with `L = 0`, always | `benchmarks/recertify.py:70–71` |\n| Cert `memo` | free, definite, subset, memo (no old move) | `learning/search_certificate.py` |\n\nThe claim covers the old-move-plus-memo combination (§2.7) and is **false** for\nthe variant bits `BM_OLD_CLOSE_COUNT` and `BM_OLD_RULE_ORDER` (§2.4). Item 06\nis to confirm both of those failures by brute force, and item 10 is to prove a\nLean counterexample to each.\n","numLines":656,"startLine":1,"totalLines":656}}}
