{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfaPfhTiHW4gGctf6Sbzv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 06 is done: the pruning rules, as written in `paper2/search_soundness.md` §2, had zero failures in the brute-force check, and both known bad forms of `better_move` fail as expected. The gate passes (1289 tests passed, `lake build` ok, still one `sorry`, the §24 conjecture) and no Lean changed.\n\n**What I built.** `paper2/search_check.py` works from the document's statements and shares no code with the solver. It has three parts:\n- an exact oracle for whether a state still has a solution;\n- a filter that runs the rules in the code's order (`definite_move → subset_rule → better_move`) and records, for every candidate it drops, which move covers it;\n- a full reimplementation of the search: free moves, memo, old move with inheritance, then the rules.\n\nRun it with `python -m paper2.search_check` (about 17 minutes on 30 cores); results go to `paper2/data/search_check.json`.\n\n**The fixed rules.**\n- **Instances:** every labelled graph on 1–6 vertices, every graph on 7 vertices under 25 labellings each, 6,000 random sparse instances at 8–16 vertices, and the three known counterexamples.\n- **Checks:** about 557 M node checks, 2.9 G cited covers, 1.9 G all-pairs checks and 83.5 M full searches. No failure in any single rule, any composition, or the \"free moves\" lemma.\n- **Old move and memo together:** this is the combination every production refutation used, and item 05 argued it is sound. All 7.3 M full searches with both on agree with the oracle. That is evidence, not proof; item 11 still has to prove it.\n- **The reimplementation matches the C:** same answer and same node count on all 4,418,084 runs, including the bug variants. So §2 describes what the code actually does, and the failures below are the C's own.\n\n**The two bad forms:**\n\n| | Bug A (close count) | Bug B (rule order) |\n|---|---|---|\n| kind of fault | the rule's own conclusion is false | every cited cover is valid, but they form a cycle |\n| first seen | 12 vertices (none up to 11) | cycles from 6 vertices; loses the last solution at a node from 8 |\n| wrong `unsat` at the optimum | only on the known 17 × 9 instance, none of the 6,000 random | from 10 vertices, with Bug B alone |\n\n**Findings:**\n- **Two new, smaller Bug B witnesses**, pinned in the tests and candidates for item 10's Lean counterexample:\n  - an 8-vertex state where better move drops 3 citing 0, the subset rule then drops 0 citing 3, and the only move left has no solution;\n  - a 10-vertex graph that Bug B alone refutes at its optimum of 3. The known 10 × 13 instance needs both bugs to give a wrong answer.\n- **The untested alternative ordering never failed.** This is the \"better move first, then subset rule without its discards\" ordering in the C (`BM_SUBSET_RESTRICTED`). It is not part of the soundness claim.\n- **The checker catches a planted fault.** Letting better move cite any candidate, not only an earlier one, fails on 60 of the 32,768 labelled 6-vertex graphs.\n\n`tests/test_search_check.py` adds 14 tests. §3 of `paper2/search_soundness.md` and PROGRESS.md (now 6/13) are updated, and the item is ticked.\n\n**Coverage limits:**\n- 7-vertex graphs were checked under 25 of their 5,040 labellings, not all of them.\n- The sets of already-searched moves were sampled when more than 4 children were refuted.\n- Bug A only shows up at 12+ customers, so it was reached by random sampling, not exhaustively."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2906,"cache_read_input_tokens":166392,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2906,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fefaae11-7851-456f-8e91-99cdb5c832da","uuid":"2c9cd3ff-cd4d-4234-b08b-130714dd73c8","timestamp":"2026-09-30T22:12:29.725Z","request_id":"req_011CfaPfgvTww2PZdceHiXfR"}
